cargo-audit checks Cargo.lock dependencies against the RustSec Advisory
Database. It also scans compiled Rust binaries for known
vulnerabilities.

Building requires rust-opt. The supplied vendor archive allows offline
compilation using the upstream Cargo.lock. Default binary scanning is
enabled. The experimental fix feature is not enabled.

Run "cargo audit" in a Rust project, or use "cargo-audit audit"
directly. Audits normally fetch the advisory database and check the
crates.io index for yanked crates. These runtime downloads are separate
from the offline package build. Run audits as your normal user,
not root.

Use "cargo audit update-db" to fetch or refresh the advisory database
without auditing a project.

For offline checks, provide an existing advisory database Git repository
with --db PATH and use --no-fetch --no-yanked. Offline results are only
as current as that database. An example configuration is installed in
/usr/doc/cargo-audit-<version>/audit.toml.example.

"cargo audit bin PATH" scans binaries. Binaries built with
cargo-auditable (optional, available from SBo) contain dependency
metadata for more complete scanning. Other binaries provide only
partial dependency data.
This tool does not perform source-level vulnerability reachability
checks.

Homepage: https://rustsec.org
Optional cargo-auditable:
https://github.com/rust-secure-code/cargo-auditable
